      "message": "Agreed that `badpages[i]` trips `CONFIG_UBSAN_BOUNDS` once `i` reaches 1, since `-fstrict-flex-arrays\u003d3` treats the one-element array as a fixed-size one, and the pointer walk avoids that here.\n\nAs far as I can see, though, this is only half of the fix: after the conversion, `setup_swap_map()` and `setup_clusters()` index `swap_header-\u003einfo.badpages[i]` the same way, for native and converted headers alike, which is what aosp/4368032 changes later in the chain. Until then, a 4KB header on a block device with two or more bad kernel pages still traps there.\n\nSince mainline still declares `badpages[1]` too, would declaring it as a flexible array in `union swap_header` (`include/linux/swap.h`) work for you instead?\n\n```\n\t\t__u32\t\tbadpages[];\n```\n\n`MAX_SWAP_BADPAGES`, `p3s_swap_header_max_badpages()` and the size of the union don\u0027t change, and every existing `badpages[i]` (here, and in `read_swap_header()`, `setup_swap_map()` and `setup_clusters()`) stops tripping the sanitizer. One generic patch (`ANDROID: mm: swap: ...`, or `FROMLIST:` if you send it upstream) could then replace this patch and aosp/4368032, without new locals in `mm/swapfile.c`.",
